# Scripts here run inside a Linux container. Checked out with CRLF on Windows,
# their shebangs become "#!/usr/bin/env bash\r" and the kernel cannot find the
# interpreter — the failure reads as "No such file or directory" naming a file
# that plainly exists. multipaz's gradlew cost us an hour of exactly this.
*.sh        text eol=lf
Dockerfile  text eol=lf

# Go toolchain and gofmt assume LF.
*.go        text eol=lf
go.mod      text eol=lf
*.cc        text eol=lf
*.h         text eol=lf

# PowerShell runs on the Windows host, so it keeps CRLF.
*.ps1       text eol=crlf

# memprofile/: the patch blocks are matched LITERALLY against LF source inside
# the container, so a CRLF checkout makes every replacement fail to find its
# site. replace.pl runs there too.
*.pl        text eol=lf
*.old       text eol=lf
*.new       text eol=lf
*.md        text eol=lf
*.csv       text eol=lf
*.txt       text eol=lf

# patches/: applied by `git apply` inside the container against LF source. A
# CRLF patch fails with "patch does not apply" on context lines that are
# byte-identical apart from the line ending, which reads as an upstream drift
# that has not happened.
*.patch     text eol=lf
